{"kind": "report", "step": 17, "ts": "2026-11-19T15:20:01.627360+00:00", "data": {"category": "dns", "host": "dns1.lan", "component": "dns_rate_limit", "cause": "The primary resolver on dns1.lan rate-limits with only 20 q/s counted over a /24 (ratelimit_subnet_len_ipv4: 24), so brief bursts from the LAN make it silently drop queries for local names, and the fallback server can't answer them either.", "evidence": ["dns1 config sets ratelimit: 20 with ratelimit_subnet_len_ipv4: 24 and rewrites *.lan/nas.lan to 10.20.0.50 \u2014 cat -n /opt/primary-dns/config.yaml", "journalctl -u primary-dns on dns1 shows three burst episodes today, e.g. 'Mar 03 09:41:00 ratelimit: 10.20.0.0/24 exceeded 20 q/s, dropping 31 queries' followed by 'back under limit' at 09:41:31 \u2014 matching alerts that occur a few times a day and self-clear within a minute or two", "dig @10.20.0.3 nas.lan answers normally (10.20.0.50, NOERROR) when the rate limiter is not in effect, so the record itself is correct", "dig @10.20.0.1 nas.lan returns NOERROR with ANSWER: 0 (empty), i.e. nothing for getaddrinfo to use \u2014 the second server clients fall back to", "gw.lan /var/unbound/unbound.conf has private-address: 10.0.0.0/8 (DNS-rebinding protection) plus forward-zone lan. -> 10.20.0.3, so the private answer coming from dns1 is discarded; drill nas.lan @127.0.0.1 on gw.lan confirms an empty ANSWER SECTION", "mon.lan /etc/resolv.conf lists nameserver 10.20.0.3 then 10.20.0.1, so a dropped query only shows up as ENOTFOUND via the fallback path; internet names go through forward-zone '.' to DoT upstreams and are unaffected"], "action": "change_config", "recommendation": "Raise the rate limit on dns1's primary-dns so normal LAN traffic fits: increase ratelimit well above the observed bursts and set ratelimit_subnet_len_ipv4 to 32 so each client is counted separately instead of the whole 10.20.0.0/24 sharing one 20 q/s budget. Secondarily, exempt the forwarded local zone (lan.) from the private-address filtering in gw.lan's unbound config so the backup resolver can still answer local names; no change is needed to the .lan record itself.", "detail": "rate limiter on primary-dns throttling the whole LAN /24", "contributing": {"host": "gw.lan", "component": "dns_resolver_config"}}}
